+# Synchronous entries in the local boundary prototype
+
+The private A2/A3 TMR prototype must keep ordinary kernels out of the mutable
+Scalar-overlap protocol. A compiler-owned entry can advertise a synchronous
+member in bits 3–5 of `scalar_prefix_tagged_args`. Absence of this proof keeps
+the conservative path. A Wait, runtime tail, or compiled-poll contract for the
+same member overrides the proof.
+
+At submission, `TaskPayload::prepare_overlap_contracts()` derives immutable
+per-member tail, prefix, scratch, and synchronous-entry descriptors. These
+descriptors occupy existing predicate-line padding; tensor/scalar offsets and
+payload size do not change. Submission refreshes all three descriptors before
+publishing each reused task slot.
+
+On dispatch, a proven synchronous entry receives `DISPATCH_ORDINARY_ENTRY` in
+the existing local-context flag. The scheduler and executor then skip the cold
+overlap state. A subsequent marked or unknown entry initializes every consumed
+field before publication, so stale state from either physical buffer cannot
+become a predecessor or completion contract. The scheduler also derives a
+marked entry's final prefix and predecessor fields before writing each once.
+
+This does not change core selection, readiness, pending dispatch, ACK/FIN
+ordering, `allow_early_resolve`, or the late-arrival notification protocol.
+The argument preparation helper is inlined into dispatch to avoid an added
+per-block call on the ordinary path. Legacy entries and profiling retain their
+existing behavior.
+
+The scheduler CPU tests exercise ordinary/marked/legacy transitions across
+both buffers and all three physical members, contradictory member metadata,
+gated consumers, unrelated local predecessors, and dispatch-ID reuse. Device
+checks additionally need the normal compiler's proof generation and full
+data/guard validation. This fix addresses ordinary-path overhead; it does not
+establish the requested marked-chain latency target. One intermittent AICPU
+error seen in a later control run is still under separate investigation.
